"[2603.23533] MDKeyChunker: What Does One LLM Call per Chunk Buy for Markdown Retrieval? (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23533)\nciteturn32185view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32184view2\",\"pattern\":\"withdrawn\"}); Total lines: 169\nL18: With qwen2.5:7b, we evaluate 79 Qasper questions over 30 papers and 73 FreshStack questions over 24 Laravel documentation files under BM25, two dense embedders, and hybrid fusion, following an analysis plan committed before results were computed. Evidence is matched only against source text, within a fixed token budget.\nL19: Under hybrid retrieval, structural chunks beat 512-character windows on both datasets (Qasper +23.0 points, 95% CI [+12.8, +33.5]; FreshStack +5.1 [+1.4, +9.0]) and 256-token windows on Qasper (+12.7 [+5.3, +20.3]) but not on FreshStack (-2.6 [-6.4, +1.2]).\nL20: Under the primary retrievers (hybrid, BM25), enrichment shows no planned-comparison difference from a free section-path prefix or from contextual retrieval; under hybrid retrieval the intervals exclude gains above 4.5 and 2.3 points on Qasper and 6.1 on FreshStack. Outside the planned comparisons, enrichment-style prefixes help BM25 on Qasper (exploratory) and mxbai on FreshStack (a secondary retriever).\nL21: Rolling keys raise key reuse from 5.5% to 14.7%, but merging does not improve retrieval, and under BM25 on Qasper merging with rolling keys scores below merging without them (-6.0 [-13.1, -0.2]). Enrichment used about 1,000 input tokens per chunk; contextual retrieval 5,520 (Qasper) and 9,825 (FreshStack). The results of versions 1 and 2 are withdrawn.\nL22: Comments:  | 32 pages. v3: new evaluation on Qasper and FreshStack (Laravel) with an analysis plan committed before results; results of v1-v2 withdrawn (Appendix F); title changed. Code, harness and results: cite9†this https URL†github.com L23: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Information Retrieval (cs.IR); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL24: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | H.3.3; I.2.7; I.7\nL25: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2603.23533 [cs.CL]\nL26:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2603.23533v3 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL27:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2603.23533†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL28: ## Submission history\nL29: \nL30: From: Bhavik Mangla [cite13†view email ]\nL31: cite5†[v1] Sun, 8 Mar 2026 07:28:53 UTC (83 KB)\nL32: cite14†[v2] Fri, 27 Mar 2026 05:05:15 UTC (82 KB)\nL33: [v3] Fri, 2 Oct 2026 18:11:56 UTC (39 KB)\nL34: \nL35: Full-text links:\nL36: \nL37: ## Access Paper:\nL38: \nL39: View a PDF of the paper titled MDKeyChunker: What Does One LLM Call per Chunk Buy for Markdown Retrieval?, by Bhavik Mangla\nL40: \nL41:   * cite7†View PDF L42:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L43:   * cite15†TeX Source L44: \nL45: cite16†view license†creativecommons.org L46: ### Current browse context:\nL47: \nL48: cs.CL\nL49: \nL50: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L51: \nL52: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2026-03 L53: \nL54: Change to browse by:\nL55: \nL56: cite22†cs L57: cite23†cs.AI L58: cite24†cs.IR L59: cite25†cs.LG L60: \nL61: ### References & Citations\nL62: \nL63:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L64:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L65:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L66: \nL67: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL68: \nL69: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL70: \nL71: [Button: ×]\nL72: \nL73: loading...\nL74: \nL75: Data provided by:\nL76: \nL77: ### Bookmark\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL80: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL81: \nL82: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL83: \nL84: Bibliographic Explorer (cite29†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL85: \nL86: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL87: \nL88: Connected Papers (cite30†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL91: \nL92: Litmaps (cite31†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL93: \nL94: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL95: \nL96: scite Smart Citations (cite32†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai )\nL97: \nL98: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL99: # Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\nL100: \nL101: [Input] alphaXiv Toggle\nL102: \nL103: alphaXiv (cite33†What is alphaXiv?†alphaxiv.org )\nL104: \nL105: [Input] Links to Code Toggle\nL106: \nL107: CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers (cite34†What is CatalyzeX?†www.catalyzex.com )\nL108: \nL109: [Input] DagsHub Toggle\nL110: \nL111: DagsHub (cite35†What is DagsHub?†dagshub.com )\nL112: \nL113: [Input] GotitPub Toggle\nL114: \nL115: Gotit.pub (cite36†What is GotitPub?†gotit.pub )\nL116: \nL117: [Input] Huggingface Toggle\nL118: \nL119: Hugging Face (cite37†What is Huggingface?†huggingface.co )\nL120: \nL121: [Input] ScienceCast Toggle\nL122: ScienceCast (cite38†What is ScienceCast?†sciencecast.org )\nL123: \nL124: [Input] Demos\nL125: # Demos\nL126: \nL127: [Input] Replicate Toggle\nL128: \nL129: Replicate (cite39†What is Replicate?†replicate.com )\nL130: \nL131: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL132: \nL133: Hugging Face Spaces (cite40†What is Spaces?†huggingface.co )\nL134: \nL135: [Input] Spaces Toggle\nL136: \nL137: TXYZ.AI (cite41†What is TXYZ.AI?†txyz.ai )\nL138: \nL139: [Input] Related Papers\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07615v1)\nciteturn32185view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32184view0\",\"pattern\":\"Settings\"}); Total lines: 795\nL237: We further demonstrate that a single vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times k}$, obtained by hashing the optimized LoRA parameters, can effectively encode the visual content of either a single image or an 81-frame 480p video. As illustrated in Fig. cite137†3(b) and Fig. cite138†3(c) , increasing the vector dimensionality $k$ consistently improves reconstruction quality. Interestingly, when the vector size $k$ is fixed, we observe that increasing the LoRA rank leads to degraded reconstruction performance.\nL238: This counter-intuitive behavior highlights a non-trivial interaction between LoRA capacity and the one-vector encoding process. We hypothesize that higher-rank LoRA adaptations introduce denser and more entangled parameter updates, which become difficult to preserve under a fixed-size hashing scheme.\nL239: ### 4.2 Perceptual Video Compression\nL240: After representing the visual signal into a single-vector adaptation, we can further quantize this vector and apply entropy coding, yielding an extremely low-bitrate visual compression framework. Under this setting, the compressed representation prioritizes to boost perceptual reconstruction quality when decoded through a powerful generative model. We refer to this compression framework as V ision (Video) in O ne V ector (VOV).\nL241: Here, we demonstrate that VOV achieves strong perceptual video compression performance compared with existing video codecs.\nL242: #### Settings\nL243: We compare VOV against representative neural video codecs, including both MSE-optimized (cite139†Jia et al., 2025 ) and perceptually optimized methods (cite140†Qi et al., 2025 ). For reference, we also report statistics from traditional video codecs, including H.265/HM (cite141†HM, 2021 ) and H.266/VTM (cite142†VTM, 2021 ). Benchmark datasets include the UVG (cite88†Mercat et al., 2020 ) and HEVC B/C/E datasets (cite87†Flynn et al., ). We adopt Wan-2.1 (1.3B) (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 ) as the base video generative model.\nL244: Due to the default generation constraints of the model, videos are processed at a resolution of $832\\times 480$ with 81 frames. For fair comparison, all benchmark videos are center-cropped and resized to $480$p, and evaluated using 81-frame clips across all methods. Training details for compression are described in Appendix cite63†E.2 .\nL245: #### Metrics\nL246: We report DISTS (cite143†Ding et al., 2020 ), FVD (cite144†Unterthiner et al., 2018 ), Pixel-Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) as primary evaluation metrics. We note that conventional pair-wise reference-based metrics such as PSNR are not well suited for the extremely low-bitrate regime considered here, as they fail to reflect perceptual quality when reconstructions are generated by stochastic generative processes. We also provide LPIPS (cite145†Zhang et al., 2018 ) results in Appendix cite65†F for completeness.\nL247: cite146†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: Comparisons with existing video codecs on UVG. For DISTS and FVD, lower is better. For PSNR, higher is better.\nL248: \nL249: cite147†Image: Refer to caption Ground Truth\nL250: \nL251: cite148†Image: Refer to caption VTM\nL252: 0.0145 bpp\nL253: \nL254: cite149†Image: Refer to caption DCVC-RT\nL255: 0.0123 bpp\nL256: \nL257: cite150†Image: Refer to caption GLC-Video\nL258: 0.0120 bpp\nL259: \nL260: cite151†Image: Refer to caption VOV\nL261: 0.0109 bpp\nL262: \nL263: cite152†Image: Refer to caption VOV + Scaling\nL264: 0.0112 bpp\nL265: Figure 5: Visual comparisons with different methods. More results are in Appendix cite67†F.2 and supplementary materials.\nL266: #### Comparisons\nL267: As shown in Fig. cite153†4 , the proposed VOV achieves strong compression performance on the UVG dataset when evaluated using perceptual metrics such as DISTS and FVD. Moreover, VOV with inference-time scaling consistently yields substantial gains with only a marginal increase in bitrate, which suggests the scaling technique improves the compression fidelity. Although the base VOV exhibits relatively low PSNR values, this does not contradict its strong perceptual performance.\nL675: Table 1: Settings for LoRA- or one-vector image representations.\nL693: Table 2: Settings for one-vector video representations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompression as Adaptation:Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2603.07615v1)\nciteturn32185view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn32184view0\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 795\nL289: We also analyze the general generation ability when using the parameterized visual memory. For the image in cite158†Figure 10 , we can see the general generation quality is largely preserved when the prompt is unrelated. However, for video, as we can see from cite160†Figure 12 , the generation can be influenced by similar objects, visual styles, or motion patterns.\nL290: This indicates that while the model is able to disentangle certain factors in the representation, it does not fully disentangle all attributes, and some remain correlated. Due to the different performance from image and video, we hypothesize that this limitation is largely due to the capacity of the base model.\nL291: ## 5 Conclusion\nL292: In this work, we propose a framework that represents a visual signal as a function describing its generation process, and compresses this function into a compact adaptation vector on top of a large-scale visual generative model. This framework leverages the knowledge learned by the pretrained generative model, enabling compact representation and efficient compression.\nL293: We further identify and demonstrate a key advantage of our approach—and, more broadly, functional representations: the function remains flexible at inference time, enabling further refinement and control.\nL294: #### Limitations\nL295: (1) The proposed framework is inherently limited by the capacity of the diffusion foundation model. As a result, we occasionally observe semantic mismatches in reconstructions, particularly for text in videos. (2) Similar to INR-based compression methods, the encoding process (i.e., overfitting the implicit representation) incurs a relatively long time. A potential future direction is to learn an amortized encoder for the vector representation, and an amortized decoder from the vector to full LoRA.\nL296: (3) The correlations between adaptation parameters are captured through a randomly assigned hashing map. Learning the amortized encoder and decoder can learn this correlation, and hence can largely improve the compression rate.\nL297: ## References\nL298:   * Albergo et al. (2023) Albergo, M. S., Boffi, N. M., and Vanden-Eijnden, E. Stochastic interpolants: A unifying framework for flows and diffusions. arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.08797, 2023.\nL299:   * Anderson (1982) Anderson, B. D. Reverse-time diffusion equation models. Stochastic Processes and their Applications, 12(3):313–326, 1982. ISSN 0304-4149. doi: https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-4149(82)90051-5. URL cite161†https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0304414982900515†www.sciencedirect.com .\nL300:   * Ballé et al. (2017) Ballé, J., Laparra, V., and Simoncelli, E. P. End-to-end optimized image compression. In 5th International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2017, Toulon, France, April 24-26, 2017, Conference Track Proceedings, 2017.\nL301:   * Ballé et al. (2018) Ballé, J., Minnen, D., Singh, S., Hwang, S. J., and Johnston, N. Variational image compression with a scale hyperprior. In 6th International Conference on Learning Representations, ICLR 2018, Vancouver, BC, Canada, April 30 - May 3, 2018, Conference Track Proceedings, 2018.\nL302:   * Brooks et al. (2024) Brooks, T., Peebles, B., Holmes, C., DePue, W., Guo, Y., Jing, L., Schnurr, D., Taylor, J., Luhman, T., Luhman, E., et al. Video generation models as world simulators. OpenAI Blog, 1(8):1, 2024.\nL303:   * Chen et al. (2021) Chen, H., He, B., Wang, H., Ren, Y., Lim, S. N., and Shrivastava, A. Nerv: Neural representations for videos. volume 34, 2021.\n"
